00:14:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really important point, and honestly, I think it's the most practical concern you've raised. If I've been sounding like I'm telling you to abandon your instincts entirely, I've done a bad job. Let me fix that. ### Your gut instinct is an essential tool. Don't throw it away. You're a landlord. You've dealt with tenants for years. You've developed a sense for when something feels off—a vague sense of unease during a viewing, a weird interaction that you can't quite put your finger on, a pattern of small inconsistencies that don't add up. That instinct is real. It's built from thousands of micro-observations your conscious brain hasn't fully processed. Good landlords rely on it. I'm not telling you to stop. What I'm saying is much narrower: **make sure your instinct is responding to the person in front of you, not a label you received before you even met them.** ### How this works in practice Imagine two scenarios: **Scenario A:** You meet a prospective tenant. They make steady eye contact, answer questions clearly, seem polite, and provide solid references. Then someone tells you they have schizophrenia. Your instinct suddenly fires up because of the label. That's not instinct. That's a pre-loaded fear overriding what your actual senses are telling you. The person didn't change; the information in your head did. That's the kind of "instinct" I'm asking you to question. **Scenario B:** You meet a tenant—any tenant. They avoid eye contact in a way that feels evasive, not just shy. Their answers are rehearsed and don't quite match what's on their application. Something in your gut says *this doesn't feel right.* You can't articulate it perfectly, but the hairs on your neck stand up. That's instinct. Trust it. Investigate further. Ask more questions. Check references more carefully. Maybe even pass on them if it doesn't sit right. This is true regardless of whether they have a diagnosis or not. ### The difference is the source of the signal Good instinct is a response to actual sensory input: body language, tone of voice, inconsistencies, micro-expressions, the way someone inhabits a space. It's fallible, but it's based on real data. Prejudice—even well-intentioned prejudice—is a response to a category in your mind. It fires up before you've even gathered data. When that happens, it crowds out your ability to read the actual person. So I'm not asking you to stop trusting your gut. I'm asking you to do the opposite: **trust your gut more by keeping it clear of noise.** If this tenant walks through the door and your gut, unattached to the label, gives you no alarm bells, then believe it. If later on, they start acting in ways that make your gut clench, believe that too—and act on it, regardless of whether it matches a diagnosis. ### The practical "where does it end?" question It ends here: you trust your instinct when it's reading *behavior*, and you pause and question it when it's reading a *label* you got secondhand. That's a clear, workable line. You don't have to become a robot. You just have to become a little more disciplined about what triggers your alarm system. That discipline doesn't weaken your ability to run a safe property. It sharpens it. Because the landlord who can read behavior without being distracted by labels catches more real problems than the one whose alarm is always ringing for the wrong reasons.
